body:
[Prop sigma-oo-pres-refl-equiv-cc of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The symmetric monoidal functor $\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty\colon\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_*^\otimes\to{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}^\otimes$ is equivariantly cocontinuous. Moreover, if ${\mathcal{C}}^\otimes$ is equivariantly presentably symmetric monoidal, then a fiberwise cocontinuous symmetric monoidal functor $F\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}^\otimes\to{\mathcal{C}}^\otimes$ is equivariantly cocontinuous if and only if $F\circ\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty_+\colon\ul{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_*^\otimes\to{\mathcal{C}}^\otimes$ is so.
    \begin{proof}
        To see that $\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty$ is equivariantly cocontinuous, we observe that each $\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty\colon{{\mathfrak S\kern-.4pt}}_{G,*}\to{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_G$ is cocontinuous (being the left derived functor of a left Quillen functor), while the Beck--Chevalley condition for left adjoints to restrictions along injective homomorphisms is the content of Lemma~(lemma:Sigma-oo-equiv-left-adjointable of the paper). This completes the proof of the first statement and the `only if' part of the second one.

        To prove the remaining direction, we left $F\colon{\mathpalette\ulhelper{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}}\hbox{\rule[-2pt]{0pt}{0pt}}^\otimes\to{\mathcal{C}}^\otimes$ be a fiberwise cocontinuous symmetric monoidal functor such that $F\circ\textbf{\textup{L}}\Sigma^\infty$ is equivariantly cocontinuous; we have to show that  the Beck--Chevalley map $i_!F\to Fi_!$ is invertible for every injective $i\colon G\to G'$. Note that the presentably symmetric monoidal $\infty$-categories ${\mathcal{C}}(G)$ and ${\mathcal{C}}(G')$ are stable as they receive symmetric monoidal left adjoints from presentably symmetric monoidal stable $\infty$-categories (namely, $F_G$ and $F_G'$); thus, the Beck--Chevalley condition follows by exactly the same reduction argument as in the proof of the previous proposition.
    \end{proof}

[Proposition 5 (Structured $(1,1,4)$ family) of arXiv:2604.10820, 'A Strict Gap Between Relaxed and Partition-Constrained\\ Spectral Compression in a Six-Sta'] Let $r\in\{1,2,3\}$, and let $\{p,q\}=\{1,2,3\}\setminus\{r\}$. For the partition obtained by
splitting $B_r$ into two singletons and merging $B_p\cup B_q$ into one four-cell,
\begin{equation}
\det Q^{(1,1,4)}_r=t_r\frac{3\ell_{rr}-1}{2}.
\end{equation}

[Lemma lem:harnack of arXiv:2604.20094, 'Persistence and local extinction for superprocesses in random environments'] There is a constant $c_{{\mathcal E}2}$ only depends on $d$ and $\Theta$ such that
    \begin{equation*}
        {\mathbf P} \left(w_a(t, x) > w_a(t, y)\mathrm{e}^{(t|x-y|)^{1 / 3}}+\mathrm{e}^{t/2-c_{{\mathcal E}2}(t|x-y|)^{-1 / 3}}\right) \leqslant \mathrm{e}^{t/2-c_{{\mathcal E}2}(t|x-y|)^{-1 / 3}} .
    \end{equation*}

[Lem 2.1-CP of arXiv:2412.00708, 'Stochastic PDE approach to fluctuating interfaces'] Let $v$ be a solution of (equation eq:1-CP of the paper).  Then,
\begin{align}
e(x):= \tfrac{\varepsilon^2}2 (v_x(x))^2-V(v(x))
\end{align}
is constant, i.e., $e(x)\equiv e$.  In particular, $v(x)$ satisfies the first order
ODE:
\begin{align}
v_x(x) = \pm \sqrt{2(V(v(x))+ e)/\varepsilon^2},
\end{align}
where $\pm$ is determined by the sign of $v_x(x)$. 
Note that $V(v(x))+e = \tfrac{\varepsilon^2}2 (v_x(x))^2 \ge 0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

