body:
[Lemma lem:core of invariant lower probabilities of arXiv:2411.02030, 'Finite ergodic components for upper probabilities'] Let $(\Omega,\mathcal{F},V,T)$ be an UPPS. Then
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item[(i)]	given any $P\in \operatorname{core}(V)$, there exists a unique $\widehat{P}\in \mathcal{M}(T)\cap\operatorname{core}(V)$ such that $P(A)=\widehat{P}(A)$ for any $A\in\mathcal{I}$;
		\medskip
		\item[(ii)] given $A\in\mathcal{F}$, if for any $P\in\operatorname{core}(V)$, $\widehat{P}(A)=0$, then $V(A)=0$.
	\end{enumerate}
	If we further assume that $V$ is ergodic, then $\mathcal{ M}(T)\cap \mathrm{core}(V)$ has only one element, denoted by $Q$, which is ergodic, and  $V\ll Q$, i.e., for any $A\in\mathcal{F}$,  $Q(A)=0$ implies that $V(A)=0$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma eq:Epi-less-m of arXiv:2604.20094, 'Persistence and local extinction for superprocesses in random environments'] Let $\rho>d$. Then $\pi_t^m$ converges to some $\pi^m$ in ${\mathcal P}({\mathcal M}_\rho({\mathbb R}^d))$. Moreover,
  	\begin{equation}
  		\int_{\mathcal{M}(\mathbb{R}^d)}\mu\pi^m(d\mu)\leqslant m.
  	\end{equation}

