{"artifact_digest":"sha256:5ebe6de9a921639101cba7a8af6df2efc6ad35c1f193712b7aa171956f3aa46d","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/5e/5ebe6de9a921639101cba7a8af6df2efc6ad35c1f193712b7aa171956f3aa46d.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:cc0a657d7e958b220009905fbbdf88a6253f0436175827d60ce9dafcf7a6b55a","bundle_entry_key":"2608-27657-theorem-8-weak-stability-of-positive-l-2","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_117","claim_kind":"theorem","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_117.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2608-27657-proposition-8-weak-stability-of-positive-l-2","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Cutoff approximations to the linear vector fields \(x\mapsto e_i x_j\)
give
\begin{equation}
  \E\tau_k(Y_k)=\E(Y_kY_k^{\mathsf T})=\Cov(Y_k).
  \tag{4.7}
\end{equation}
By \cref{lem:stein-fourth},
\[
  \sup_k\E|Y_k|^4\leq9nL.
\]
Thus \((|Y_k|^2)_k\) is uniformly integrable.  Weak convergence implies
convergence of the first and second moments, and therefore
\begin{equation}
  W_2(\mu_k,\mu)\longrightarrow0.
  \tag{4.8}
\end{equation}

Choose a subsequence along which the liminf in \eqref{eq:stable-basic} is
attained.  The joint laws of \((Y_k,\tau_k(Y_k))\) are tight.  Passing to
a further subsequence, let
\[
