[Proposition 10 (Secular equation for the eigenvalues) of arXiv:2511.12623, 'The eigenvector bead process'] If $g_j=0$ for some $j$, then $\lambda^{(N)}_j$ remains an eigenvalue of $H_{N+1}$ with eigenvector
$(u^{(N)}_j,0)$. All other eigenvalues of $H_{N+1}$ are the simple zeros of the secular function
\begin{equation}
f(z):=g_{N+1}-z-\sum_{j=1}^N \frac{|g_j^{(N)}|^2}{\lambda^{(N)}_j-z}
\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 50 of arXiv:2609.03403, 'Hyperpositive functions, sector bounded functions and a trace formula'] $F\in\mathcal{SB}_T$ if and only if there exists a $\mathbb C^{m\times m}$-valued bounded function $S$ such that
  \begin{equation}

    F(z)=-T+T\left(I_m-T^{-1/2}S(z)(T+T^{-1})^{-1/2}\right)^{-1},
  \end{equation}
  if and only if there exists a $\mathbb C^{m\times m}$-valued bounded function $\sigma$ such that
  \begin{equation}

    F(z)=T^{-1}+(T+T^{-1})^{1/2}\sigma(z)T^{-1/2}.
    \end{equation}

