[Lemma 4 of arXiv:2608.26509, '2608.26509'] Let \(A\geq0\) be self-adjoint on a real or complex Hilbert space \(H\).
Suppose that \(U:(0,\infty)\to H\) is locally absolutely continuous, has a
strong limit \(U(0)\) at zero, and satisfies the following conditions:
\begin{enumerate}
 \item \(U(y)\in\cD(A^{1/2})\) for almost every \(y>0\);
 \item the map \(y\mapsto A^{1/2}U(y)\), defined arbitrarily on the
 exceptional null set, is strongly measurable;
 \item the extension energy \(\mathfrak E_s(U)\) defined in
 \eqref{eq:extension-energy} is finite.
\end{enumerate}
Then \(U(0)\in\cD(A^{s/2})\) and
\begin{equation}
 \|A^{s/2}U(0)\|_H^2\leq C_s\mathfrak E_s(U).

\end{equation}

[Proposition prop: limiting measure of y-intervals of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] For each $\omega \in \Gamma$, there exists a nonnegative function $\map{\theta(\omega)}{\mathbbm{R}}{\mathbbm{R}_+}$ such that $\theta(\omega) \in L_{loc}^1(\mathbbm{R})$ and the following property holds:
	\begin{equation}

		\bar\boldsymbol{\nu}_t\left(\omega, [y_1, y_2]\right) = \lim_{N \to \infty} \bar\boldsymbol{\nu}_t^N\left(\omega, [y_1, y_2]\right) \leq \int_{y_1}^{y_2} \theta(\omega, s - t)ds
	\end{equation}
	for all $t \in \mathcal{C}(\omega)$ and $0 \leq y_1 \leq y_2$. Moreover,
	\begin{equation*}
		\bar\boldsymbol{\nu}_t\left(\omega, [y_1, y_2]\right) \leq \int_{y_1}^{y_2} \theta(\omega, s - t)ds
	\end{equation*}
	for all $t \geq 0$ and $0 \leq y_1 \leq y_2$, i.e., even if $t \notin \mathcal{C}(\omega)$. Hence, the measures $\bar\boldsymbol{\nu}_t$ are absolutely continuous with respect to the Lebesgue measure for all $t \geq 0$ with probability one.

