body:
[Lemma estimate_tau of arXiv:2603.05491, '\textbf{Local limits of uniform triangulations with boundaries in high genus}'] There exists a constant $C > 0$ such that for all $n,g \ge 0$ with $n \ge 2g-1$,
\begin{align*}
    e^{-Cn} n^{2g} \le \tau(n,g) \le e^{Cn} n^{2g}.
\end{align*}

metadata_json:
{"artifact_digest":"sha256:687c74072361d7b766868b783caf2f0ce3e79d73ed3b507fb6f9ee3662f46c72","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/68/687c74072361d7b766868b783caf2f0ce3e79d73ed3b507fb6f9ee3662f46c72.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:5fe61c4a662b4c1757fa2113406412a70097b4367196008a241511a820a6f715","bundle_entry_key":"2603-05491-lemma-10-estimate-tau","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_101","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_101.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2603-05491-lemma-10-estimate-tau","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Let $\widetilde{\mathcal{T}}(n,g)$ denote the set of triangulations $t \in \mathcal{T}(n,g)$ endowed with a spanning tree $S$. 
Since $t$ has $n + 2 - 2g$ vertices, the tree $S$ has $n + 1 - 2g$ edges. 
Write $\tilde{\tau}(n,g)$ for the cardinality of this set. 
For each $t \in \mathcal{T}(n,g)$, since $t$ has exactly $3n$ edges, the number of spanning trees of $t$ is at most $\binom{3n}{n + 1 - 2g} \le 8^n$. 
Hence
\begin{align*}
    8^{-n} \tilde{\tau}(n,g) \le \tau(n,g) \le \tilde{\tau}(n,g).
\end{align*}

We now estimate $\tilde{\tau}(n,g)$. 
