[Theorem thm:fBm peturbed lattice of arXiv:2602.19773, 'Regularization of a stationary point process by a stationary increments perturbation'] Let $\xi$ be a stationary point process with intensity $1$ and finite second moment, \textit{i.e.}
		\begin{equation*}
			\@ifnextchar[{\@withe}{\@withoute}{\xi(K)^{2}}<+\infty, \quad K \text{ compact }, 
		\end{equation*}
		and $\hat{\xi}$ be its Palm distribution. If $B$ is a $d$-fBf independent of $\hat{\xi}$, then 
		\begin{enumerate}[(i)]
			\item $\hat{\xi}_{B} = \{x + B_{x} : x \in \hat{\xi}\}$ is the Palm distribution of an ergodic point process $\xi_{B}$ with intensity $1$,
			\item the Bartlett's spectrum of $\xi_{B}$ is absolutely continuous with respect to Lebesgue measure and its density (also known as \emph{structure factor}) is given by
			\begin{equation}
				s_{\xi_{B}}(t) = \@ifnextchar[{\@withe}{\@withoute}{\int_{\mathbb{R}^{d}}e^{-\frac{1}{2}(\Sigma_{x}t,t)}e^{-i( t,x)} \, \hat{\xi}(\mathrm{d}x)}, \quad t\neq 0,
			\end{equation} 
			which converges absolutely on all compact subsets of $\mathbb{R}^{d}\backslash\{0\}$. Here, $\hat{\xi}$ is identified with a discrete measure.
		\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

