[Theorem thm:Gaussian:moments of arXiv:2605.28106, 'Gaussian Processes with Sample Paths in Reproducing Kernel Banach Spaces'] Let \( \gamma \) be a Radon Gaussian measure on \( (X,\mathcal{B}(X)) \).
    Then
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item There exists a unique element \( a \in X \) such that
        \begin{equation*}
            f(a) = \int_X f(x) \, \gamma(dx), \qquad \forall f \in X^*.
        \end{equation*}

        \item There exists a unique linear operator \( R : X^* \to X \) such that
        \begin{equation*}
            \langle Rf,g \rangle =
            \int_X (f(x)-a_\gamma(f))(g(x)-a_\gamma(g)) \, \gamma(dx)
            \qquad \forall f, g \in X^*
        \end{equation*}
    \end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

