{"artifact_digest":"sha256:ee59b357bbed26c9f68079c21b5de5ae94481ec3a8e0487be96abf5d5d1ada93","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/ee/ee59b357bbed26c9f68079c21b5de5ae94481ec3a8e0487be96abf5d5d1ada93.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:b532e2bbf2b2f67db6368ab06758d6330c929232a22b31dabc29807738896d7e","bundle_entry_key":"2411-10113-lemma-20-single-concentration-stable","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_099","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_099.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2411-10113-lemma-20-single-concentration-stable","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Given $u,s$ with $1<u<s$, take $q := {\underline{q}_\alpha}(u,s)>0$ where ${\underline{q}_\alpha} (u,s)$
is defined in equation~(equation eq:q-lower-bound of the paper). Then there exist $\varepsilon_0>0$ and  $K \in {\mathbb N}$ (both depending on $q$ and hence on $u$ and $s$) such that 
\begin{equation}
\left(q^{-1} +K\varepsilon\right) \cdot \left( q - \varepsilon \right) > (1 + \varepsilon) ,\text{ for all } \varepsilon \in (0,\varepsilon_0).
\end{equation}
Define $k_x = \lceil (q^{-1} + K\varepsilon) (s-1)x \rceil$, as in the lemma, for this choice of~$K$.
Take $\varepsilon \in (0,\varepsilon_0)$.
