{"artifact_digest":"sha256:9b1315a49a50efdde00e8ce810fbb0bbe9848abd8d4a127dc8a25c82d920efe0","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/9b/9b1315a49a50efdde00e8ce810fbb0bbe9848abd8d4a127dc8a25c82d920efe0.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:9fa96be7daac2e8c42b566b1c2ec599eab42a5942f381b366fc358fa7a0bdf88","bundle_entry_key":"2507-01406-lemma-13-pcm-invariance","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s0_020","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s0_020.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2507-01406-lemma-13-pcm-invariance","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Write $X=(B_{1},\ldots ,B_{m})$, where 
\begin{equation*}
B_{r}=(X_{2r-1},X_{2r}).
\end{equation*}
By PCM, the blocks are independent and each block is bivariate
countermonotone.

The \textit{Lorenz step} consists of two operations. First one tilts the law
by the weight 
\begin{equation*}
\prod_{i=1}^{d}X_{i}=\prod_{r=1}^{m}X_{2r-1}X_{2r}.
\end{equation*}
This weight factorizes over the blocks. Hence the tilted law still has
independent blocks. Inside each block, tilting by $X_{2r-1}X_{2r}$ does not
