body:
[Definition 2 of arXiv:2609.03003, 'Causal Foundation Models'] A DGP satisfies the \textbf{ignorability} assumption if for all $t \in \mathcal{T}$,
\begin{equation}
    Y(t) \independent T \mid X.
\end{equation}
This is also called the (conditional) unconfoundedness assumption. It says that conditional on $X$, treatment assignment is independent of the potential outcomes. Individuals who actually received treatment $t$ are representative, with respect to $Y(t)$, of individuals who could have received treatment $t$. This allows us to identify the distribution of potential outcomes from observed factual outcomes because ignorability implies
\begin{equation}
    P(Y(t) \mid X = x) = P(Y \mid T=t, X=x).
\end{equation}

