[Definition 46 (The on/off Brownian snake) of arXiv:2604.01852, 'The on/off Brownian snake'] Consider finite measures $\mu_{m d}$ and $\mu_{m a}$ on $\mathbb{R}^d$. Let $(\Omega, \mathcal{F}, \mathbb{P}_{(\mu_{m d},\mu_{m a})})$ be a probability space, let $\mathbb{E}_{(\mu_{m d},\mu_{m a})}$ be the expectation operator, and suppose there exists on this space a Poisson random measure $\sum_{i \in I} \delta_{f^i}$ on the space of excursions in $\mathbb{R}_+$ with intensity measure $(
orm{\mu_{m d}} + 
orm{\mu_{m a}})n(\cdot)$, where $n$ is the It\^o excursion measure for Brownian motion, for which we can mark the excursions in the following way. For $i \in I$:
     egin{enumerate}
        \item with probability $
orm{\mu_{m d}}/(
orm{\mu_{m d}} + 
orm{\mu_{m a}})$ we mark $f^i$ with $(b_i,s_i)$ where $b_i$ is sampled from the normalised probability distribution $\Tilde{\mu}_d \coloneqq \mu_{m d}/
orm{\mu_{m d}}$ and $s_i$ is sampled independently from the $	ext{Exp}(\Tilde{c})$ distribution (the exponential distribution with density $\Tilde{c}e^{-\Tilde{c}z}$, $z>0$);
        \item with probability $
orm{\mu_{m a}}/(
orm{\mu_{m d}} + 
orm{\mu_{m a}})$ we mark $f^i$ with $(b_i,s_i)$ where $b_i$ is sampled from the normalised probability distribution $\Tilde{\mu}_a \coloneqq \mu_{m a}/
orm{\mu_{m a}}$ and $s_i = 0$.
    \end{enumerate}
    We then mark the pair $((b_i,s_i),f^i)$ with a snake $x^i$ where the snake is sampled from the probability distribution $\mathbb{P}_{(b_i,s_i)|f^i}(\cdot)$. Let
     egin{equation}
        \sum_{i \in I} \delta_{((b_i,s_i), f^i, x^i)}
    \end{equation}
    be the Poisson random measure we obtain. We call this \emph{the on/off Brownian snake (with initial condition $(\mu_{m d}, \mu_{m a})$)}.

