[Lemma 12 (Single-target order criterion) of arXiv:2608.16701, 'Order-Sensitive Fast-Synapse Limits in Sparse Excitatory--Inhibitory Threshold--Reset Netw'] The excitatory-first and inhibitory-first families produce different firing
counts on $[0,4\varepsilon]$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{x+a-b<\theta\le x+a.}

\end{equation}
In that case the excitatory-first target fires once and the
inhibitory-first target does not fire.

[Proposition 5 ({\cite[Lemma 3.11) of arXiv:2607.18104, '2607.18104'] {GZ:fractal}}]
There exist constants $c,C>0$, such that for all  $\phi>0$,
\begin{align*}
&\P\Bigg(\TF_{s+\theta^{-1}}(\pi) \geq \phi \theta^{-2/3}\log^{3}\big(1+\theta\lVert (x,s;y,t)\rVert_2\big),\,\,\,\forall(x,s;y,t)\in \R^{4}_{\uparrow},\pi\in \mathfrak{G}_{(x,s),(y,t)}, \theta\geq 2(t-s)^{-1}\Bigg)\\
&\leq C\exp\big({-c \phi^{9/4}}\big). 
\end{align*} By symmetry, the result holds with $s+\theta^{-1}$ replace by  $t-\theta^{-1}$. Hence,  
\begin{equation}
\begin{split}

&\P\Bigg(\TF(\pi) \geq \phi  |t-s|^{2/3}\log^{3}\bigg(1+\frac{\lVert (x,s;y,t)\rVert_2}{|t-s|}\bigg),\,\,\,\forall (x,s;y,t) \in \R^{4}_{\uparrow},\pi\in \mathfrak{G}_{(x,s),(y,t)}\Bigg)  \\
&\leq C\exp({-c \phi^{9/4}}).   
\end{split}

\end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm see \cite{KM21} thm (\textcolor{red}{2.5}) of arXiv:2505.14762, 'Multiple radial SLE($\kappa$) and quantum Calogero-Sutherland system'] Under the neutrality condition $\left(\mathrm{NC}_b\right)$, the value of the differential $C_{\mathbb{D}}\left[\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{+}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-}\right]$ in the identity chart of $\mathbb{D}$ is given by
\begin{equation}
C_{\mathbb{D}}\left[\boldsymbol{\sigma}^{+}, \boldsymbol{\sigma}^{-}\right]=\prod_{j<k}\left(z_j-z_k\right)^{\sigma_j^{+} \sigma_k^{+}}\left(\bar{z}_j-\bar{z}_k\right)^{\sigma_j^{-} \sigma_k^{-}} \prod_{j, k}\left(1-z_j \bar{z}_k\right)^{\sigma_j^{+} \sigma_k^{-}},
\end{equation}
where the product is taken over finite $z_j$ and $z_k$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Thm GirsanovHTransform of arXiv:2411.13765, 'Schr\"odinger Bridge Problem for Jump Diffusions'] Assume that \textup{(Assumption:SDEsolution of the paper)} holds for the SDE (equation ReferenceLevyIto of the paper) and $(\left\{ X_t \right\}_{[0,T]},\mathbf{P})$ is the solution to (equation ReferenceLevyIto of the paper) that is a strong Markov process with $X_0\sim\rho_0$. Let $h$ be a non-negative harmonic function and satisfies the mean-value property (equation Equation:MeanValueProperty of the paper). 
    Let $\left\{ Z_t \right\}_{[0,T]}$ be the process given by 

    \begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned} Z_t:=\exp\bigg(-\int_0^tu(s)dB_s-\frac{1}{2}\int_0^t&|u(s)|^2ds+\int_0^t\int_{\mathbb{R}^\ell}\log(1-\theta(s,z))\tilde{N}(ds,dz) \\    &+\int_0^t\int_{\mathbb{R}^\ell}\left[\log(1-\theta(s,z))+\theta(s,z)\right]\nu(dz)ds\bigg)
 \end{aligned}
\end{equation}
     with
    \begin{equation}
        \begin{cases}
            u(t):=-\mathbbm{1}_{\set{\tau_n^0\geq t\textup{ ev.}}}\sigma^T\nabla\log h(t,X_{t})\textup{ for }t\in[0,T], \\
            \displaystyle \theta(t,z) := 1-\mathbbm{1}_{\set{\tau_n^0\geq t\textup{ ev.}}}\frac{h(t,X_{t-}+\gamma(t,X_{t-},z))}{h(t,X_{t-})}\textup{ for }t\in[0,T],\,z\in\mathbb{R}^\ell,
        \end{cases}
    \end{equation}  
    where  $\set{\tau_n^0\geq t\textup{ ev.}}:=\bigcup_{n\geq 1}\left\{ \tau_n^0\geq t \right\}$ for $\left\{ \tau_n^0 \right\}_{n\geq 1}$ being the sequence of stopping times defined in (equation def_tau_n of the paper). Then, $\left\{ Z_t \right\}_{[0,T]}$ is a $\left\{ \mathcal F_t \right\}$-local-martingale under $\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{\left\{ X_0\in A_0 \right\}}}{r_0}\mathbf{P}$. 

   Further, if $\mathbf{P}^h$ is the $h$-transform of $\mathbf{P}$ as defined in (equation Definition:h-transform,general case with tau0 of the paper), then \begin{equation}  \mathbf{P}^h=Z_T\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{\left\{ \tau_0>T \right\}}}{r_0}\mathbf{P}.
    \end{equation}
   Hence if $\mathbf{P}(\tau^0>T)=r_0$, then $\mathbf{P}^h$ is the Girsanov transform of $\frac{\mathbbm{1}_{\left\{ \tau^0>T \right\}}}{r_0}\mathbf{P}$ with the above coefficients $u(t),\theta(t,z)$.

