{"artifact_digest":"sha256:aa2b61952436ce402e6c5ec5d214ef12c9a3d293bae77541d53ff14f5d777411","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/aa/aa2b61952436ce402e6c5ec5d214ef12c9a3d293bae77541d53ff14f5d777411.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:14fcb619c3e845da9375d6fb85c7ade57b8577f5d930c65cb728b651aa4250ef","bundle_entry_key":"2609-02064-lemma-8-textsf-quenched-switching-at-t","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s3_146","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-20260907/bundle_s3_146.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2609-02064-lemma-8-textsf-quenched-switching-at-t","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: The argument is quenched.  Fix a realization of the Brownian
  environment for which all the Brownian paths are continuous.  We
  first treat the case $y<x$.  The case $y>x$ will follow by
  interchanging the two paths.

  For this proof, it is convenient to use the normalization
    $\cZ_z^{\al,\be}
    =
    \int_{\DN}
      e^{\be H_z^\al(\ga)}\,\mu_\be(d\ga)
    =\be^n Z_z^{\al,\be}.$
  Then
  \begin{align*}
    Q_z^{\al,\be}(d\ga)
    =
    \frac{e^{\be H_z^\al(\ga)}}{\cZ_z^{\al,\be}}
    \,\mu_\be(d\ga).
  \end{align*}
