body:
[Lemma 16 of arXiv:2608.18075, '2608.18075'] There is a measurable random radius $r_*(x,\omega)>0$ with the following
property. If $X'_0=x'$ satisfies $(x,x')\in\mathscr U_\Delta$ and
\begin{align*}
\|\Delta_x(x')\|_{E_x}<r_*(x,\omega),
\end{align*}
define recursively
\begin{align*}
h_n=\Delta_{X_n}(X'_n),\qquad
X'_{n+1}=\Phi\bigl(X'_n,Y_n-\Gamma K_q(X_n,Y_n)h_n\bigr).
\end{align*}
Then $(X_n,X'_n)\in\mathscr U_\Delta$ for every $n$, the recursion is
well defined, and
\begin{align}
\|h_n\|_{E_{X_n}}\le C(x,\omega)e^{-\lambda n}\|h_0\|_{E_x},
\qquad
\sum_{n\ge0}\|h_n\|_{E_{X_n}}^2<\infty.
\end{align}
Consequently $d_{\mathsf X}(X_n,X'_n)\to0$ exponentially.

If $D\subset\mathsf X$ is a set on which $\mathcal W$ is bounded, then
there are deterministic $r_0,p_0>0$ such that
\begin{align}
\inf_{x\in D}\mathbf P_x\{r_*(x,\omega)\ge r_0\}\ge p_0.
\end{align}

