body:
[Theorem perfect_1 of arXiv:2411.10502, 'Strategies in a mis\`{e}re two-player tree search game'] Assuming that both players play optimally, and know this about each other, the probability that player $1$ wins in $MS(P_n)$ is given by
\begin{equation}

	1/2\textrm{ when n is even;}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}

	\frac{2k}{4k+1}\textrm{ when }n = 4k+1 \textrm{ for some integer k;}
\end{equation}
\begin{equation}

	\frac{2k+2}{4k+3}\textrm{ when }n = 4k+3\textrm{ for some integer k.}
\end{equation}

Further, the guesses that achieve these maximum winning probabilities are described as follows.

\begin{enumerate}
\item When $n = 4k$ for some $k,$ player 1 should choose a number that divides the game into ranges of sizes $4k_1 + 1$ and $4k_2 + 2,$ for any choice of $k_1, k_2$ with $k_1 + k_2 = k - 1.$
\item When $n = 4k + 1$ for some $k,$ all of player 1's choices are of equal strength.
\item When $n = 4k + 2$ for some $k,$ player 1 should choose a number that divides the game into ranges of sizes $4k_1 + 1$ and $4k_2,$ for any choice of $k_1, k_2$ with $k_1 + k_2 = k.$
\item When $n = 4k + 3$ for some $k,$ player 1 should choose a number that divides the game into ranges of sizes $4k_1 + 1$ and $4k_2 + 1,$ for any choice of $k_1, k_2$ with $k_1 + k_2 = k.$
\end{enumerate} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem thm:LLN of arXiv:2605.06887, 'Law of large numbers for activated random walk on villages'] Given $V$,  $P$, $\lambda$ as in Section (ss_vm_jk_sr of the paper) and initial conditions
$\sigma$ and $\nu$ (cf.\ (equation eq_sigma_nu_initial of the paper)) that also satisfy $\sigma_x \leq \frac{\lambda_x}{1+\lambda_x}$ for all $x \in V$, the system of equations
\begin{eqnarray} 

s_x  & = & - m_x + \sigma_x +\nu_x + \sum_{y} m_y P_{y,x}, \qquad  x \in V \\ 

s_x & = & \sigma_x e^{-(\nu_x + \sum_y m_y P_{y,x})} + \frac{\lambda_x}{1+\lambda_x} \left(1-e^{-(\nu_x + \sum_y m_y P_{y,x})}\right),  \qquad  x \in V, \end{eqnarray}
has a unique solution $s_* \in [0,1]^V$ and $m_* \in \mathbb{R}_+^V$ and the random vectors $s_*^{(n)}$ and $m_*^{(n)}$ converge in probability to the deterministic limits $s_*$ and $m_*$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem:stopped_Z of arXiv:2608.06263, 'Almost sure path localisation for the derivative martingale of branching Brownian motion'] Let $(\mathcal{L}_t,\tau_t)_{t \ge 0}$ be a non-decreasing family of stopping lines such that, for any $x \in \mathbb{R}$, the following two conditions hold $\mathbb{P}_x$-almost surely:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item any stopping line is anterior to some generation: for any $t \ge 0$, there exists $s \ge 0$ such that $(\mathcal{L}_t,\tau_t) \preceq (\mathcal{N}_s,s)$.
        \item any generation is anterior to some stopping line: for any $s \ge 0$, there exists $t \ge 0$ such that $(\mathcal{N}_s,s) \preceq (\mathcal{L}_t,\tau_t)$.
    \end{enumerate}
    Then, the stopped martingale $Z_{\tau_t}$ converges almost surely to $Z_\infty$ as $t \to \infty$. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 3 of arXiv:2605.28801, 'Microscopic Weak Selection Principle for the Logistic Branching Brownian Motion with selec'] Given the Log-BBM($1,c$) $(\boldsymbol{X^c}(t))_{t\geq 0}$,
	\begin{itemize}
		\item[i)]  there exists $v_{c} \in \mathbb{R}$ such that for every $\xi_{N_0^c}$,   
		\begin{equation*}
			\lim_{t\rightarrow\infty} t^{-1}\max_{u\in\mathcal{N}^c_t} X^c_u(t) = \lim_{t\rightarrow\infty} t^{-1}\min_{u\in\mathcal{N}^c_t} X^c_u(t) =v_{c} \quad \mathbb{P}_{\xi_{N_0^c}}\text{-a.s and in } \ L^1.
		\end{equation*}
		\item[ii)]         Furthermore, we have that 
		\begin{equation*}
			v_0:=\lim_{c\rightarrow0} v_{c} = \sqrt{2}.
		\end{equation*}   
	\end{itemize} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem 6 of arXiv:2606.27520, 'Critical curve of loop percolation on the $d$-regular tree'] If $\kappa > \kappa_c$, then
    \begin{equation} 
        \E_{\alpha,\kappa} \big[ |\cC(0) | \big] \asymp \frac{1}{\alpha_c - \alpha}, \quad \text{ as } \alpha \uparrow \alpha_c,
    \end{equation}
    and
    \begin{equation}
         \theta(\alpha, \kappa) \asymp \alpha-\alpha_c, \qquad \text{as } \alpha \downarrow \alpha_c. 
    \end{equation}
    In the case $\kappa=\kappa_c$,
    \begin{equation}
        \theta(\alpha, \kappa_c) \asymp \alpha^2, \qquad \text{as } \alpha \downarrow \alpha_c =0. 
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem T:Main-Result of arXiv:2606.23865, 'Uniform-in-time Gaussian fluctuations for multiscale nonlinear stochastic systems via Mall'] Let $\theta_t^\varepsilon \triangleq \frac{X_t^\varepsilon-\bar{X}_t}{\sqrt{\varepsilon}}$ and $N \sim \mathscr{N}(0, \sigma_t^2).$ Assume that Assumptions (A:Boundedness of sigma-tau of the paper) through (A:eta-eps-interaction of the paper) hold. Then, for sufficiently small $\varepsilon$ and $\eta$, there exists a (time-independent) positive constant $K$ such that
\begin{multline*}
\begin{aligned}
\sup_{t \ge 0} d_W(\theta_t^\varepsilon, N) & \le  K \sqrt{\sqrt{\eta + \varepsilon} + \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} - \frac{1}{\gamma^2} \right)}  + K \sqrt{ \varepsilon + \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} (\sqrt{\varepsilon}+ \sqrt{\eta})}\\
 & \qquad \qquad \quad + K \left[ \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{1}{2}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} + \varepsilon^{\frac{1}{4}}  +  \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{1}{8}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} + \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right) \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} + \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{1}{4}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}} +  \left( \frac{\eta}{\varepsilon} \right)^{\frac{5}{8}} \eta^{\frac{1}{4}}  \right].
 \end{aligned}
\end{multline*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Defin 22 of arXiv:2604.03548, 'Degree-preserving conservative processes and a unified approach for their hydrodynamics'] A probability generator is a linear operator $\mathcal L$ on $C(\Omega)$ satisfying the following properties: 
\begin{itemize}
\item[\textnormal{(a)}] The domain $\mathcal D(\mathcal L)$ is dense in $C_0(\Omega)$. 
\item[\textnormal{(b)}] If $f\in \mathcal D(\mathcal L)$, $\lambda\ge0$ and $g=f-\lambda \mathcal Lf$, then,
\begin{equation*}
\inf_{\eta\in \Omega} f(\eta) \ge \inf_{\eta\in \Omega} g(\eta).  
\end{equation*}
\item[\textnormal{(c)}] $\mathcal R(\lambda-\mathcal L)$ is dense in $C_0(\Omega)$ for all sufficiently small $\lambda>0$. 
\item[\textnormal{(d)}] If $\Omega$ is compact, $1\in \mathcal D(\mathcal L)$ and $\mathcal L1=0$. On the other hand, if $\Omega$ is not compact, for small $\lambda >0$ there exists a sequence $\{f_n\}_{n\in\mathbb N}\subset  \mathcal D(\mathcal L)$ such that $g_n=f_n-\lambda \mathcal Lf_n$ satisfies $\sup_{n}\|g_n\|<+\infty$, and both $f_n$ and $g_n$ converge to 1 pointwise.   \end{itemize}

[Lemma lem:lsv-tail of arXiv:2607.06249, 'Extreme least singular values of Gaussian row submatrices and a phase retrieval stability '] Let $G\in\mathbb{F}^{m\times m}$ have independent standard Gaussian entries, where $\mathbb{F}\in\{\mathbb{R},\mathbb{C}\}$ and
$d_\mathbb{F}=\dim_\mathbb{R}\mathbb{F}$. There is a constant $C_\mathbb{F}$ such that, for all $m\ge1$ and all $t>0$,
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{P}\{\sigma_{\min}(G)\le t\}\le C_\mathbb{F} m^{1+d_\mathbb{F}/2}t^{d_\mathbb{F}}.
\end{equation}

