[Definition def:nSD of arXiv:2411.15401, 'The reference interval in higher-order stochastic dominance'] [{\color{black}[SS07]}]
Let $n\in\mathbb{N}$. For $X,Y\in\mathcal X$, we say that $X$ dominates $Y$ in the sense of $n$th-order stochastic dominance on $\mathbb{R}$ ($n{\rm SD}_{\mathbb{R}}$), denoted by $X\ge_{n} Y$ or $F_X\ge_{n} F_Y$ if 
\begin{align*}
F_X^{[n]}(\eta)\le F_Y^{[n]}(\eta),~\forall \eta\in\mathbb{R}
\end{align*}
or equivalently,
\begin{align*}
\mathbb{E}[(\eta-X)_+^{n-1}]\le \mathbb{E}[(\eta-Y)_+^{n-1}],~\forall \eta\in\mathbb{R}.
\end{align*}

[Proposition prop:full-cm-extension of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] For a potential-realizable response satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \overline{H_{\mathrm{reg}}}^{H}=H,
 \qquad
 \int_{S}f\,d\rho=0\quad(f\inH_{\mathrm{reg}}),

\end{equation}
the map $f\mapstoB_f^\Lambda(\mu)$ extends uniquely from $H_{\mathrm{reg}}$ to a
continuous linear map on $H$, characterized in any orthonormal basis
$(\phi_i)_{i=1}^r$ of $E_\mu(\Lambda)$ by
\begin{equation}
 \left(\inner{B_f^\Lambda(\mu)\phi_j}{\phi_i}
              _{L^2(\mu)}\right)_{i,j=1}^r
 =-\kappa\Lambda
 \left(\inner{U_\mu\Theta_\mu(\phi_i,\phi_j)}{f}_{H}
  \right)_{i,j=1}^r,
 \qquad f\inH.

\end{equation}
For $f\notinH_{\mathrm{reg}}$, this notation refers only to the continuous extension;
no analytic perturbation path is asserted.

[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2606.22526, 'The conditional expectation of the product of the first $n-1$ Hermite polynomials in a mul'] Let the $n-$dimensional random vector $\mathbf{X\allowbreak=\allowbreak(}
X_{1},\ldots,X_{n})^{T}$ be normaly distributed with $N(\mathbf{0,\Sigma)}$,
with positive definite matrix $\mathbf{\Sigma\allowbreak=\allowbreak}\left[
\rho_{j,k}\right]  $ with $\rho_{j,j}\allowbreak=\allowbreak1,$
$j,k\allowbreak=\allowbreak1\ldots,n$. This means that all marginal
$1-$dimensional distribution are $N(0,1)$.

1. The density of this distribution can be expanded in the following infinite
series
\begin{equation}
f_{mN}(x_{1}\ldots,x_{n}|\mathbf{\Sigma)=}\prod_{j=1}^{n}f_{N}(x_{j}
)\sum_{k_{1},\ldots,k_{n}}c_{k_{1},\ldots,k_{n}}\prod_{j=1}^{n}H_{k_{j}}
(x_{j}). 
\end{equation}

2. The coefficients of this expansion are given by equation
\begin{equation}
c_{k_{1},\ldots,k_{n}}\prod_{j=1}^{n}k_{j}!=E\left(  \prod_{j=1}^{n}H_{k_{j}
}(X_{j})\right)  
\end{equation}

3. Moreover, the coefficients $c_{k_{1},\ldots,k_{n}}$ take on non-zero values
if $\sum_{j=1}^{n}k_{j}$ is even and for all $j\allowbreak=\allowbreak
1,\ldots,n$
\begin{equation}
k_{j}\leq\sum_{s\neq j}k_{s}. 
\end{equation}

