Let $\sigma_i^2=S_{ii}$.  Mills' bounds imply, for every $\sigma\geq0$,
\[
  \frac{\overline\Phi(t/\sigma)}{\overline\Phi(t)}
  \longrightarrow
  \begin{cases}
    0,&0\leq\sigma<1,\\
    1,&\sigma=1,\\
    +\infty,&\sigma>1,
  \end{cases}
\]
where the numerator is understood to be zero for $t>0$ when $\sigma=0$.
If some $\sigma_i>1$, then
$\mathbb P\{M_Y>t\}\geq\mathbb P\{Y_i>t\}$, contradicting
(equation eq:maximum-tail-first-order of the paper).  ...","release_manifest_digest":"sha256:d33212ded62ab36e5facb74ceda5fe0888d59a355c1078f387a571243d82b031","revision_digest":"sha256:562d52051fbec1421be601ad95e97ea7ad65e3243908ac941457e1522115ad0e","source_id":"arxiv:2607.14087","source_revision":"tex-as-downloaded-2026-09-08","statement_fidelity_reviewed":false,"supersedes":[],"trust_class":"curated_secondary","truth_status":"unverified"}

