[Defn def-hm of arXiv:2506.18827, 'Random walk reflected off of infinity, with applications to uniform spanning forests and s'] Let $\mathcal A\subset\mathcal V\mathcal G$ be a finite set of vertices. 
For $y \in \mathcal A$, let $\mathbbm 1_y : \mathcal A \to\mathbbm R$ be the function which equals 1 at $y$ and zero elsewhere. 
By Proposition~(prop-min-harmonic of the paper), there exists a unique energy-minimizing discrete harmonic function $h^y   : \mathcal V\mathcal G \to \mathbbm R$ with $h^y|_{\mathcal A} = \mathbbm 1_y$. For $x\in \mathcal V \mathcal G$, we define the \textbf{(energy-minimizing) harmonic measure} on $\mathcal A$ viewed from $x$ by 
\begin{equation}   
\operatorname{hm}_{\mathcal A}^x(y) := h^y(x) , \quad\forall y \in \mathcal A .
\end{equation}

[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2609.01540, '2609.01540'] There is \(c\in\mathbb R\) such that
\begin{equation}
 v-c=o_{2,\alpha}(r^{1-n/2}).

\end{equation}
If, in addition,
\(a-a_\infty=O_{1,\alpha}(r^{-\varepsilon})\) for some small \(\varepsilon>0\), then
there are \(b\in\mathbb R\) and \(\varepsilon_0>0\) such that
\begin{equation}
 v=c+b\rho_{a_\infty}^{2-n}
 +O_{2,\alpha}(r^{2-n-\varepsilon_0}).

\end{equation}

