{"artifact_digest":"sha256:cd865f14dd30882ced4827a47177e3c661980ec995cd594490394e82b5de44a2","artifact_path":"objects/sha256/cd/cd865f14dd30882ced4827a47177e3c661980ec995cd594490394e82b5de44a2.md","bundle_digest":"sha256:3bd0b9ae7b8c1442b1762df3ae0535e0d47e2e0d87623f5e34abd84fc8464e9c","bundle_entry_key":"2607-04514-lemma-11-admissibility-of-the-growing-c","bundle_id":"population.tex-extract-s1_040","claim_kind":"lemma","conditions":[],"domain":"probability theory","draft":"tex-extract-v2-20260907/bundle_s1_040.json","evidence_ids":[],"logical_id":"math:harvest.arxiv.mathematics:arxiv-2607-04514-lemma-11-admissibility-of-the-growing-c","overlay_schema_version":1,"proof_text":"Paper's own proof, opening: Since the function \(\vartheta_\sigma\) does not depend on \((a,x)\), only \(\sigma\)-derivatives
have to be estimated. The case \(j=0\) is straightforward from \(|\vartheta_\sigma|\leqslant 1\).
For \(1\leqslant j\leqslant s\), repeated differentiation of \(\vartheta( \sigma^{1/4}\zeta)\)
produces a finite sum of terms of the form
\begin{equation*}
  \sigma^{-j}\, P_{j,\mu}(\sigma^{1/4}\zeta)\, \partial^\mu\vartheta(\sigma^{1/4}\zeta),
