body:
[Theorem thm:mainexplicit of arXiv:2606.06187, 'How to Study Reflected Brownian Motion in a Quadrant via Kernel Functional Equations?\\ \m'] For all $y\in \mathcal{D}_\mathcal{R}$, the Laplace transform $\widehat \pi_1$ is given by
\begin{equation}

\widehat \pi_1(y)
=C  \left( \frac{w(0)-w(p)}{w(y)-w(p)} \right)^{-\chi}
 \exp\left(\frac{1}{2i\pi} \int_{\mathcal{R}^-} \log G(t) \left[ \frac{w'(t)}{w(t)-w(y)}
\right]
\mathrm{d}t\right),
\end{equation}
where $C>0$ is an explicit constant,
$p$ is the possible pole of $\widehat \pi_1$,
$\chi$ equals $0$ or $-1$ depending on the parameters, $\mathcal{R}^-$ is the half with negative imaginary part of the hyperbola $\mathcal{R}$ defined in (equation eq:curve_definition1 of the paper), and $w$ is the conformal gluing function defined in~(equation eq:expression_CGF_BM1 of the paper). (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

