[Lemma 21 of arXiv:2604.25336, 'Central limit theorem for slow-fast system under mixed fractional Brownian motion'] Let \(p\ge 2\) and \(\gamma\in(\frac{1}{2},H)\). Under \textbf{H1}--\textbf{H3}. As \(\epsilon\) tends to zero, there exists a \(\kappa>0\) such that
\begin{eqnarray}
\|x^\epsilon-\bar x\|_{\gamma,p} \le C_{x_0,T} \epsilon^\kappa 
\end{eqnarray} 
where \(C_{x_0,T}>0\) only depends on \(x_0\) and \(T\) and \(\bar x\) is the solution to the Young SDE \eqref{averaged-system}.
Moreover, \(\bar g\) is globally Lipschitz continuous. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma 7 of arXiv:2609.02779, '2609.02779'] There is $C>0$ such that every lift of a $u_0$-calibrated curve
$Z_0:(-\infty,0]\to T^*M$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \int_{-\infty}^0 d_{T^*M}(Z_0(s),\widetilde{\A})\,\dd s\le C,
\end{equation}
and every lift of a $u_\lambda$-calibrated curve
$Z_\lambda:(-\infty,0]\to T^*M$ satisfies
\begin{equation}
 \int_{-\infty}^0 e^{\lambda s}
 d_{T^*M}(Z_\lambda(s),\widetilde{\A})\,\dd s\le C
\end{equation}
for all sufficiently small $\lambda>0$.

[Lemma of arXiv:2511.05223, 'Kac's Program and Relative Entropy Decay for Nonlinear Spin-Exchange Dynamics'] Let \(\mu_t=S_t(\mu)\) be solution of (equation non-linear rqs of the paper) with initial datum $\mu$.
If \(NMLSI(\alpha)\) holds, then 
\begin{equation}
    H(\mu_t|\mu') \le e^{-\alpha t} H(\mu|\mu'),
\end{equation}
where \(\mu'\) is the stationary state obtained as the unique element of  \(\ensuremath{\mathcal P}_J^\ensuremath{\mathcal A}(\Omega)\) such that \(\r^\ensuremath{\mathcal A}(\mu)=\r^\ensuremath{\mathcal A}(\mu')\).

[Lemma lem:recursion-fnk-new of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] The arrays $(F^s_{n,k})_{n,k\geq0}$ corresponding to adjacent values of $s\in\{0,\dots,d-1\}$ are linked by the following relations:
	\begin{align}
	F^s_{n,k}
	&=\sum_{j\geq 0}W_{j} \,F^{s-1}_{n,k+j},
	\qquad &&s\in\{1,\dots,d-1\},\\
	F^0_{n,k}
	&=\sum_{j\geq 0}W_{j} \,F^{d-1}_{n-1,k+j-1},
	\qquad &&s=0,
	\end{align}
	for all $s,n,k$ such that $nd+s\geq 2$ and $kd+s\geq1$.
	To give sense to (equation eq:relation-fnk-different-s-new of the paper) when $s=1$ and $k=0$, we make the convention that $F^0_{n,0}=0$ for all $n\geq1$.

