[Corollary cor:lbm-finite-energy-simple-response of arXiv:2608.02459, 'Response Calculus for Spectral Simplicity\\ and Joint Eigenvalue Densities'] Let $x\in\Omega_{\rm LBM}$, let $\Lambda_n^x$ be a simple eigenvalue
of $A_x$, and choose a real $L^2(M_x)$-normalized eigenfunction $\phi_n^x$.
The response measure $\eta_n^x$ in
(equation eq:lbm-spectral-response-measure of the paper) belongs to $\mathcal M_{\rm fe}(D)$ and satisfies
\begin{equation}
 I_D(\eta_n^x)
 \le \gamma^2(\Lambda_n^x)^4M_x(D)^{1/2}
       K_G(x)^{3/2}<\infty.

\end{equation}
The simple-eigenvalue response functional extends continuously to $H$ as
\begin{equation}
 f\longmapsto
 -\gamma\Lambda_n^x\int_D\widetilde f(\phi_n^x)^2\,dM_x
 =\inner{U_D\eta_n^x}{f}_{H},
 \qquad f\inH.

\end{equation}

[Thm 2 (Theorem 4.1, \cite{LP17}) of arXiv:2605.31568, 'Lipschitz continuity of the time constant for continuum percolation'] \emph{[Palm theory for Poisson processes.]}
For all non-negative bounded and measurable functions $f$ we have \begin{align*}
\E\left[\sum_{x_1,x_2,...,x_k \in X_\lam} f(x_1,x_2,...,x_k,X_\lam) \right] &= \lam^k \int_{\R^d} \E\left[f(x_1,...,x_k,X_\lam \cup \{x_1,...,x_k\}) \right]\,dx \ \\
&= \lam^k \int_{\R^d} \E^{\lam}_{x_1,...,x_k}\left[f(x_1,...,x_k,X_\lam) \right]\,dx. 
\end{align*} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Proposition 11 of arXiv:2609.02426, 'Deformations of the $\osp(n|2)$-action on the superspace\\ of symbols of differential oper'] Let $1\leq k\leq m$ and put $a^k_1=\Delta_1A_k$, $a^k_2=\Delta_2A_k$. Then, as cochains on
$\osp(n|2)$,
\begin{equation}

a^k_2=h_k,\qquad a^k_1=-\bigl(h_k+\widetilde{h}_k\bigr),
\end{equation}
i.e.\ for every $F$ such that $X_F\in\osp(n|2)$,
\begin{equation}

h_k(X_F)=(-1)^{n|F|}F'A_k,\qquad
h_k(X_F)+\widetilde{h}_k(X_F)=A_k\circ(F'\cdot).
\end{equation}
In particular $a^k_1$ and $a^k_2$ are $1$-cocycles whose classes form a basis of
$\Hd^1(\osp(n|2),\Dc^n_{\lambda_k,\mu_k})\simeq\R^2$, and
\begin{equation}

\delta^{\sigma,\tau}A_k=\sigma a^k_1+\tau a^k_2 .
\end{equation}

