[Lemma 5 of arXiv:2604.10051, 'Ergodicity of the voter model with dynamic anti-voter bonds'] Fix~$k \in \N$. Then, for every~$\eta \in \Omega_\mathrm{site}$ and~$(\mathbf x, \mathbf i ,A,B) \in \mathcal S_k$ we have that
    \begin{equation}
        \lim_{t \to \infty} P_{\mathbf x,\mathbf i,A,B}( \eta(X^1_t) = \mathsf i^1_t, \dots, \eta(X^k_t) = \mathsf i^k_t \mid A_t,B_t, \;(\mathbf X_s)_{s \ge 0} ) = \frac{1}{2^k} \; \text{ a.s.}
    \end{equation} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Prop he-preserve-but-he-also-reflec of arXiv:2608.28504, 'Global $\bm\infty$-categories and global Thom spectra'] The functor $\textup{diag}\colon\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Sp}\to\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}$ preserves and reflects $G$-global weak equivalences.
    \begin{proof}
        We may equivalently show that the induced functor ${\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-lvl}\to{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-gl}$ precisely inverts the $G$-global weak equivalences.

        That it inverts all $G$-global weak equivalences was established in Corollary~(cor:diag-preserve of the paper). For the converse, let $f\colon X\to Y$ be any $G$-global weak equivalence in ${\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-lvl}$ and consider the naturality square
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                X\arrow[d,"f"']\arrow[r,"\eta"] & iLX\arrow[d,"iLf"]\\
                Y\arrow[r,"\eta"'] & iLY
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        for the unit of the Bousfield localization $L\colon{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-lvl}\rightleftarrows{\mathfrak d\kern-.25pt}{\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$}^\Omega
	\nobreak
	\mspace{6mu plus 1mu}
	{:}
	\nonscript\mkern-\thinmuskip
	\mathpunct{}
	\mspace{2mu}
 i$. As we have just seen, $\textup{diag}$ sends the horizontal maps to equivalences in ${\mathfrak S\kern-.5pt\mathfrak p}_\text{$G$-gl}$. Thus, if $\textup{diag}\,f$ is a $G$-global weak equivalence, then so is $\textup{diag}\,iLf$ by 2-out-of-3; conversely, to show that $f$ is a $G$-global weak equivalence, it will suffice to show that $iLf$ is so. In other words, we may assume without loss of generality that $X$ and $Y$ are $G$-global $\Omega$-spectra. After cofibrant-fibrant replacement in the $G$-global level model structure, we may then assume that $f$ is represented by a map $X\to Y$ in $\cat{$\bm G$-$\textbf{\textup{L}}$-Sp}$ between cofibrant-fibrant $G$-global $\Omega$-spectra; we will denote this pointset level map by $f$ again, and we will similarly view $\textup{diag}\,f$ as a map in $\cat{$\bm{G}$-OrthSp}$.

        By the previous corollary, both $\textup{diag}\,X$ and $\textup{diag}\,Y$ are $G$-global $\Omega$-spectra, so that $\textup{diag}\,f$ is a $G$-global \emph{level} weak equivalence. We will now complete the proof of the proposition by showing that $f(V)$ is an $({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)$-global \emph{level} weak equivalence of orthogonal $({\textup{O}}(V)\times G)$-spaces for any $V$, i.e.\ $f(V)(W)$ is a $\mathcal G_{H,G}$-equivariant weak equivalence for every compact Lie group acting arbitrarily on $V$ and faithfully on $W$. To prove this, we consider the commutative diagram
        \[
            \begin{tikzcd}
                X(V)(W)\arrow[d,"f(V)(W)"']\arrow[r,"X(V)(i)"] &[1.5em] X(V)(V\oplus W)\arrow[d,"f(V)(V\oplus W)"{description}]\arrow[r,"\tilde\sigma(V\oplus W)"]&[2em]\Omega^WX(V\oplus W)(V\oplus W)\arrow[d,"\Omega^Wf(V\oplus W)(V\oplus W)"]\\
                Y(V)(W)\arrow[r,"Y(V)(i)"'] & Y(V)(V\oplus W)\arrow[r,"\tilde\sigma(V\oplus W)"'] & \Omega^WY(V\oplus W)(V\oplus W)
            \end{tikzcd}
        \]
        By Lemma~(lemma:I'm-so-static of the paper), the horizontal maps are $\mathcal G_{H,G}$-weak equivalences. On the other hand, $f(V\oplus W)(V\oplus W)$ is a $\mathcal G_{H,G}$-weak equivalence by assumption on $\textup{diag}\,f$, whence so is $\Omega^Wf(V\oplus W)(V\oplus W)$ by Lemma~(lemma:smash-lQ of the paper). The claim follows by 2-out-of-3.
    \end{proof} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Definition of arXiv:2506.21466, 'The Markov property for $\varphi^4_3$ on the cylinder'] The approximate $\varphi^4_3$ measure on $M^\sigma$ with pair of boundary conditions $\varphi^{0,\sigma}_-,\varphi^{0,\sigma}_+ \in S'(\mathbb T^2)$ is the probability measure $\nu_T^\sigma(\cdot \mid \varphi_-^{0,\sigma},\varphi_+^{0,\sigma})$ with Radon-Nikodym derivative
\begin{equation}
\nu_T^\sigma(d\varphi \mid \varphi_-^{0,\sigma}, \varphi_+^{0,\sigma}) = \frac{1}{\mathcal Z_T^\sigma(0\mid \varphi_-^{0,\sigma},\varphi_+^{0,\sigma})} \exp(-V_T^\sigma(\varphi \mid  \varphi_-^{0,\sigma}, \varphi_+^{0,\sigma})) \mu^\sigma(d\varphi),
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal Z_T^\sigma(0\mid \varphi_-^{0,\sigma},\varphi_+^{0,\sigma})$ is a normalization constant. For every $f \in C^\infty(M^\sigma)$, the unnormalized Laplace transform of $f$ under this measure is the quantity
\begin{equation}
\mathcal Z^\sigma_T(f \mid \varphi^{0,\sigma}_-,\varphi^{0,\sigma}_-):=	\mathbb E_{\mu^\sigma} \left[ \exp\left(\langle f,\varphi\rangle_{M^\sigma} -V_T^\sigma(\varphi \mid \varphi^{0,\sigma}_-,\varphi^{0,\sigma}_+) \right) \right], \qquad \forall f \in C^\infty(M^\sigma),
\end{equation}
where $\langle \cdot,\cdot \rangle_{M^\sigma}$ denotes the $L^2(M^\sigma)$ inner product. Note that taking $f=0$ above indeed yields the normalization constant $\mathcal Z^\sigma_T(0 \mid \varphi^{0,\sigma}_-,\varphi^{0,\sigma}_+)$.

[Lemma lem:11 of arXiv:2512.09219, 'Strong confluence of geodesics in Liouville quantum gravity'] Fix $z\in \mathbb{C}, r>0$. On the event $F_r(z)$, the following hold.
  \begin{enumerate}
  \item  Any path $\xi\subseteq B_{\delta_2 r}(V_r(z))$ from $B_{\delta_2 r}(I_u)$ to $B_{\delta_2 r}( (\mathcal{M}\setminus S_u)\cup S_v\cup \mathcal{A}_{v_+}\cup \mathcal{A}_{v_-})$ must necessarily intersect $B_{\delta_2 r}(\mathcal{A}_{u_-}\cup \mathcal{A}_{u_+})$.
  \item Any path $\xi\subseteq B_{\delta_2 r}(V_r(z))$ from $B_{\delta_2 r}(I_v)$ to $B_{\delta_2 r}( (\mathcal{M}\setminus S_v)\cup S_u\cup \mathcal{A}_{u_+}\cup \mathcal{A}_{u_-})$ must necessarily intersect $B_{\delta_2 r}(\mathcal{A}_{v_-}\cup \mathcal{A}_{v_+})$.
  \end{enumerate}

[Mylem 8 (Corollary 1.4 in \cite{RV_imrn}) of arXiv:2607.00495, 'Rank deficiency of Bernoulli random matrices for growing corank'] Consider a random vector $X = (X_1,...,X_n)$ where $X_i$ are real-valued independent random variables. Let $t, b \ge  0$ be such that
\begin{align*}
    \mathcal{L}\left( X_{i}, t \right) \le b \text{ for all } i \in [n]
\end{align*}
 Let $P$ be an orthogonal projection in $\mathbb{R}^{n}$ onto a $d$-dimensional subspace. Then
 \begin{align*}
     \mathcal{L}\left( PX, t\sqrt{d}  \right) \le \left( C_{\ref{Small ball probability for linear image}}b \right)^{d},
 \end{align*}
 where $C_{\ref{Small ball probability for linear image}}>0$ is a absolute constant. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

