body:
[Lem 97 of arXiv:2605.26664, 'Mixing times for Glauber dynamics of lozenge tilings of the hexagon'] We have w.o.p. that the following statements hold for all $t \in [\mathsf{T}', N^5]$. 

  \begin{enumerate} 
  	\item We have that $y_{a,b,c}^{\pm,j} \le \mathsf{y}_t^{\pm, j}$ for each $j \in [1,k]$.  
  	\item We have that $\widetilde{U}_k (x) \le \mathtt{U}_t^{k/N} (x)$ for each $x \in [x^-, x^+]$. 
  \end{enumerate}

[Lemma 12 (Four-point correlation bound) of arXiv:2604.00692, 'Uniform-in-time diffusion approximations for multiscale stochastic systems'] Let $f\in L^\infty(\mT^d)$ be centered such that $\mu(f)=0$. For any ordered times $0 \leq r_1 \leq r_2 \leq r_3 \leq r_4$, the unperturbed diffusion $X$ satisfies
\begin{equation*}
\big|\mE^0\big[f(X_{r_1}) f(X_{r_2}) f(X_{r_3}) f(X_{r_4})\big]\big|
\leq C \|f\|_\infty^4\,\e^{-\kappa(r_2-r_1)}\e^{-\kappa(r_4-r_3)},
\end{equation*}
where $\kappa>0$ is the spectral gap constant from \eqref{eq:exp-erg}.

[Cor TP2-forests-partition-function of arXiv:2411.03065, 'Growing conditioned BGW trees with\\ log-concave offspring distributions'] If ${\mathbf{w}}$ is log-concave, then the array $(\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n,k})_{n,k\geq1}$ is TP2, that is
	\begin{align}
	\forall 1\leq {n}\leq{n'},\quad \forall 1\leq{k}\leq{k'},\qquad
	\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n,k}\,\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n',k'}
	\geq \PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n,k'}\,\PFforests{{\mathbf{w}}}{n',k}.
	\end{align} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lem lem-ptwise-conv of arXiv:2506.18827, 'Random walk reflected off of infinity, with applications to uniform spanning forests and s'] Almost surely, the following is true. Let $\eta\in\Xi$, let $\widehat\eta$ be its successor, and let $K$ be a compact subset of $[ \tau_\eta, \tau_{\widehat\eta})$. 
For each large enough $n \geq n_z$ (how large is random and depends on $\eta$), we have 
\begin{equation}  
X^n_t = X_t ,\quad\forall t \in K . 
\end{equation} 
In particular, for each fixed $t\geq 0$, a.s.\ $X^n_t = X_t$ for each sufficiently large $n\geq n_z$.

