\]
Here $C_t>0$ is a constant depending on $t$, $Z$ is a standard normal random variable and ${\rm dist}$ is the 1-Wasserstein distance, the Fortet-Mourier distance, or the Kolmogorov distance. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Lemma lem-2m-recurrent of arXiv:2510.13733, 'Internal Diffusion Limited Aggregation \\ with Critical Branching Random Walks'] Assume $d=2$.
Then there exists a finite absolute constant $C_2$ 
such that for any $R>0$ and any $z\in \partial B_R$
\begin{equation}
\sum_{y\in B_R} G_R(0,y)G_R^2(y,z)\le C_2.
\end{equation}

[Lemma 8 of arXiv:2607.11756, 'Full replica symmetry breaking in the Sherrington--Kirkpatrick model'] Let \(\phi\in C^\infty(\R)\) be even and strictly convex.  Assume that
\begin{equation}
 -1<\phi'(x)<1,
 \qquad
 |\phi(x)|\le C+|x|,
\end{equation}
that \(\phi^{(j)}\) is bounded for every \(j\ge1\), and that
\begin{equation}
 \phi(x)=x+c+d_1\e^{-2x}+d_2\e^{-4x}+d_3\e^{-6x}
 +O(\e^{-8x}),
 \qquad d_1>0,
\end{equation}
as \(x\to+\infty\), with differentiated remainder of every order.  Fix \(a>0\) and \(r\ge0\), and let
\[
 u(x)=T_{a,r}\phi(x).
\]
Then \(u\) is even, belongs to \(C^\infty(\R)\), is strictly convex, and satisfies
\begin{equation}
 -1<u'(x)<1,
 \qquad
 |u(x)|\le C_{a,r}+|x|.
\end{equation}
Every derivative \(u^{(j)}\), \(j\ge1\), is bounded.  Moreover, there exist constants
\(\widetilde c,\widetilde d_1,\widetilde d_2,\widetilde d_3\) such that
\begin{equation}
 u(x)=x+\widetilde c+\widetilde d_1\e^{-2x}
 +\widetilde d_2\e^{-4x}+\widetilde d_3\e^{-6x}
 +O(\e^{-8x}),
\end{equation}
with differentiated remainder of every order, and
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde d_1=d_1\e^{2(1-a)r}>0.
\end{equation}

If \(u(r,x)=T_{a,r}\phi(x)\) and \(r\) is restricted to a compact interval, then the coefficients in \eqref{eq:u-tail} are smooth functions of \(r\).  For every \(\alpha,\ell\ge0\), the expansion obtained by applying \(\partial_r^\alpha\partial_x^\ell\) to \eqref{eq:u-tail} has a remainder that is uniform for \(r\) in that interval.

