body:
[Proposition prop: existence of solutions of arXiv:2608.23756, 'Measure-valued fluid limits for partial service queues\\with correlated patience and servi'] Let $\rho \in \mathcal{M}_F^+(\mathbbm{R}_+^2)$ be a probability measure that is stochastically smaller than the probability measure with density $g$. Specifically, 
	\begin{equation*}
		\angled{\phi}{\rho} \leq \int_{\mathbbm{R}_+^2} \phi(x, y)g(x, y)dxdy
	\end{equation*}
	for each Borel $\map{\phi}{\mathbbm{R}_+^2}{\mathbbm{R}}$ that is nondecreasing in the componentwise order and bounded. Also, suppose that $\rho$ satisfies (b) of Definition (def: fluid problem of the paper). Then, for each constant $\alpha \geq 0$, there exists a solution $\boldsymbol{\mu}$ of the fluid problem with initial condition $\boldsymbol{\mu}_0 = \alpha \rho$. If the variables $x$ and $y$ represent the attained service and time spent in the system for tasks present at time zero, respectively, then the interpretation of the probability measure $\rho$ is that $\rho(A)$ represents the fraction of tasks present at time zero for which $(x, y) \in A$.

[Theorem 113 (Paley–Zygmund inequality) of arXiv:2507.07702, 'Observables of random spanning trees in random environment'] If $Z \geq 0$ is a random variable with finite expectation, then for any $0 \leq t \leq 1$
    \begin{equation*}
        \P \big( Z > t \E[Z] \big) \geq (1- t)^2 \frac{\E[Z]^2}{\E[Z^2]}.
    \end{equation*}

