[Theorem thm:gumbel of arXiv:2607.01463, 'Two Multi--Draw Coupon Collector models with different retention rules'] A collector receives at each run $d$ distinct coupons ($d$ is a positive integer) and keeps \textbf{all the new} observed coupons (\textit{Problem I}), while he chooses \textbf{the least--collected coupon} at each run (\textit{Problem II}). Let $T_{N,d}$ and $T_{N,1}$ be the random variables denoting, respectively, the number of trials for a complete set of $N$ different types of coupons, which are uniformly distributed. Then, as $N\to\infty$,
\begin{equation}
    \frac{
        T_{N,d}
        -
        \frac{N}{d}\log N
    }{
        \frac{N}{d}
    }
  \Longrightarrow
    G, 
\end{equation}
and
\begin{equation}
    \frac{
        T_{N,1}
        -
        \frac{N}{d}\log N
        -
        N C_d
    }{
        \frac{N}{d}
    }
    \Longrightarrow
    G,

\end{equation}
where
\[
    C_d
    =
    \int_0^1
    \left[
        \frac{1}{1-(1-x)^d}
        -
        \frac{1}{dx}
    \right]\,dx,
\]
and $G$ is the standard Gumbel random variable, with distribution function
\[
    \mathbb{P}(G\le x)=\exp\{-e^{-x}\},
    \qquad x\in\mathbb{R}.
\] (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

