body:
[Corollary 3 (In-expectation consequence and sharp global scale) of arXiv:2606.07931, 'Pointwise Complexity for Gaussian Fields:\\Upper Envelopes, Algorithmic Lower Bounds, and '] Under the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:pointwise-gaussian}, there exists an absolute constant \(C>0\) such that
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E\left[
\sup_{x\in T}
\frac{
\bigl(|X_x|-C\max\{\Phi_\mu(x),r_0\}\bigr)_+
}{
\max\{\sigma(x),s_0\}
}
\right]
\le
C\sqrt{\log(eM_{r_0,s_0})}.
\end{equation}
Consequently, for every \(x\in T\),
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E|X_x|
\le
C\max\{\Phi_\mu(x),r_0\}
+
C\max\{\sigma(x),s_0\}\sqrt{\log(eM_{r_0,s_0})}.
\end{equation}
At the global level, taking \(r_0\ge\Phi_{\mu,\ast}\) and \(s_0=R_\ast\) gives \(M_{r_0,s_0}=1\), hence
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E\sup_{x\in T}|X_x|
\le
C(r_0+R_\ast).
\end{equation}
Letting \(r_0\downarrow \Phi_{\mu,\ast}\), then optimizing over \(\mu\), and using \eqref{eq:absolute-global-optimality}, we obtain the sharp equivalence
\begin{equation}
\mathbb E\sup_{x\in T}|X_x|
\asymp
R_\ast+\inf_{\mu\in\Delta(T)}\Phi_{\mu,\ast}.
\end{equation}
Thus the optimized expectation version is optimal up to absolute constants. (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

[Theorem theo:minimaxgan of arXiv:2411.10235, '\textbf{Smooth transport map via diffusion process}'] Let $n\in \mathbb{N}_{>0}$, $\beta> 0$, $p^\star \in \mathcal{F}_\beta$ and $(X_1,...,X_n)$ an i.i.d. sample of law $p^\star$. The GAN estimator $\hat{g}$ (equation eq:gan of the paper) using the classes of functions $\mathcal{G}$ (equation eq:G of the paper) and $\mathcal{D}$ (equation eq:D of the paper), satisfies for all $\gamma>0$
\begin{align*}
    \sup_{p^\star \in \mathcal{F}_\beta}\ \mathbb{E}_{X_i}[d_{\mathcal{H}^{\gamma}_1}(\hat{g}_{\# }\gamma_d,p^\star)] =\tilde{O}\left( \inf_{\hat{\theta}\in \Theta}\ \sup_{p^\star\in \mathcal{F}_\beta}\ \mathbb{E}_{X_i}[d_{\mathcal{H}^{\gamma}_1}(\hat{\theta},p^\star)]\right),
\end{align*}
where $\Theta$ denotes the set of all possible estimators  of $p^\star$ based on n sample.

