[Theorem weak_convergence_alpha_quantile of arXiv:2605.20471, 'Note on the Weak Convergence of Hyperplane $α$-Quantile Functionals and Their Continuity i'] Let $\alpha \in (0,1),t>0,\gamma \in \mathbb{R}^d$. Let $X^n\Rightarrow X$. Suppose that for all $\varepsilon >0$ we have, alternatively:
	\begin{enumerate}
		\item $P(M_{t,\beta}(X)>M_{t,\alpha}(X)+\varepsilon)\to 0$ as $\beta \downarrow \alpha$ or,
		\item $P(X \in C_{\mathbb{R}^d}[0,\infty))=1$;
	\end{enumerate}
	Then $M_{t,\alpha}(X^n)\to^{\textnormal{w}}M_{t,\alpha}(X)$ in the usual sense.

[Lem lem-1d-soln of arXiv:2411.06301, 'Inverting the operation of conditioning a branching process on extinction'] Let $k\in \mathbbm N$ such that $\mathbbm P_k[X_k(1) \geq 2] > 0$, which in particular holds for $k=1$. There exists a unique $\eta_k  \in (0,\infty)$ satisfying 
\begin{equation}   
g_k(0,\dots, 0,\eta_k , 0 ,0,\dots) = \eta_k 
\end{equation} 
where here the input of $g_k$ has 0s in all components but the $k$th, which is $\eta_k $. 
Furthermore, for each $d\geq k$, 
\begin{equation}  
\lambda_k^d \leq \eta_k  .
\end{equation}  
Finally, if $t > 1$ is as in Assumption~(item-gf-perturb-infty of the paper) and $\phi,\psi$ are as in Assumption~(item-gf-growth-infty of the paper), then for all integers $d \geq k \geq 1 $, 
\begin{equation}  
\lambda_k^d \leq \phi(k) \log t + \log\psi(k)  .
\end{equation}

