body:
[Theorem thm_brownrec of arXiv:2411.09976, 'Occupation times on the legs of a diffusion spider'] Let  $\mathbf{X}$ be a Brownian spider and let $A_1^{(i)}$ be the occupation time on leg~$L_i$ up to time~1. 
For any $r\in\{1,\dotsc,R\}$ and $n_1,\dotsc,n_r \geq1$, 
\begin{equation} 
\mathbf{E}_\mathbf{0}\left(\prod_{i=1}^r (A_1^{(i)})^{n_i}\right) = \sum_{k_1=1}^{n_1} \dotsm \sum_{k_r=1}^{n_r} 2^{-(2N-K-1)} \frac{\Gamma(K)}{\Gamma(N)} \prod_{j=1}^r \frac{\Gamma(2n_j-k_j)\,\beta_j^{k_j}}{\Gamma(k_j)\Gamma(n_j-k_j+1)} , 
\end{equation}
where $N=n_1+\dots+n_r$ and $K=k_1+\dots+k_r$.

