[Theorem thm_uniform_known of arXiv:2608.09105, 'Longest convex chains with i.i.d.\ points'] {ambrus_barany09}}] 
Let $\mathcal{U}_n$ be a set of $n$ independent uniform samples from $\mathcal{T}$.
Denote the longest convex chain in $\mathcal{U}_n$ by
\eq{
\mathscr{L}_n^\mathsf{unif} \coloneqq \max\{\#\mathcal{C}:\, \text{$\mathcal{C}\subseteq\mathcal{U}_n$ and $\mathcal{C}$ is a convex chain}\}.
}
There exists a constant $\alpha\in(0,\infty)$ such that
\eq{
\lim_{n\to\infty}\frac{\mathbb{E}\mathscr{L}_n^\mathsf{unif}}{n^{1/3}} = \alpha.
} (Proof in the paper; no proof environment follows the statement in the TeX.)

